#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221110 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e3f0f2d-2ca7-59a7-801f-3ff937510d61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With One Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "6649e376-1865-5cb9-8892-687f80082d22" :question/math-academy-id "e-21650" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-2x(t) + 6y(t); y^{′}(t) = x(t) - 3y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2=-5$, with corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [3, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-2, \\ 1]^T$, respectively. Using this information, sketch the phase portrait of the system." :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}$. These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac13x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/04a7ef2e0aa4f7e78b2e2d6bcd3ecc67.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y=-\\dfrac12x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since $e^{-5t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/29bacb4b190583c38b6f6c1e63da2895.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$. So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/c788bfa30548a6e1dcaf080f3d6fcaf0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221111 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4dd289f-95e4-5d51-8544-d20e3f5b158c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "e89c64cb-e046-592c-929b-9269cb7b2758" :question/math-academy-id "e-21651" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-3x(t) + 9y(t); y^{′}(t) =-x(t) + 3y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $\\mathbf{v} = [3, \\ 1]^T$. The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [-1, \\ 0]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}$. Sketch the phase portrait of the system." :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}$, the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v} te^{\\lambda t} + \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\,)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3t - 1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nThese solutions form the line with the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac13x\n$$\n\nin the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/1d5dfee4e3ecc9de1abd7b5c988c3572.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nFor example:\n\n- When $c_1=1$ and $c_2=1$, we get the following curve:\n${x = 3t + 2; y = t + 1$\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/b2eab0b163b394484f17ad33df6c1d82.png)\n\n- When $c_1=-1$ and $c_2=-1$, we get the following curve:\n${x =-3t - 2; y =-t - 1$\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/7555ac8c4917b8f00d3dddd94a3a9e7c.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/9c4709214d5c0674d33a227c8b36885c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221112 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e50fd4a2-76cd-5de5-b461-28a07c4f98bc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/id #uuid "edb1633f-44e1-5324-8048-80252c65afe5" :question/math-academy-id "e-21654" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 2x(t) + 2y(t); y^{′}(t) = 5x(t) + 5y(t)$\nFind an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)\n$$\n\nare the points for which\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{2x + 2y = 0; 5x + 5y = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+2y=0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 2y=-2x \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad y=-x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e3f0f2d-2ca7-59a7-801f-3ff937510d61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With One Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8b6cf67-3d1e-5744-a8e6-da1c8170f063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/q-332864-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220272} {:answer/id #uuid "bf31f7b3-c296-5426-8458-e7e895bcca86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/q-332864-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220273} {:answer/id #uuid "5a6bf095-ae26-5a33-b083-a2fd6d398548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/q-332864-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220274} {:answer/id #uuid "74879ec7-005c-52b4-9440-a0e73e746238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/q-332864-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220275} {:answer/id #uuid "7acc10dc-f981-54eb-a2a4-7e31eaa6e103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/q-332864-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7acc10dc-f981-54eb-a2a4-7e31eaa6e103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/q-332864-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220276} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2816e62-0076-5c76-a781-fbd6eb41715b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220271}] :question/id #uuid "059b4cf7-aecd-509d-b95b-c42c513302b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-332864" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = x(t) + y(t); y^{′}(t) = 2x(t) + 2y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1} = 0$ and $λ_{2} = 3$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $v_{1} = [- 1, 1]^{T}$ and $v_{2} = [1, 2]^{T}$, respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e3f0f2d-2ca7-59a7-801f-3ff937510d61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With One Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94d0846d-9483-577c-bd27-28dac6977b07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/q-333937-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220573} {:answer/id #uuid "a90eb8e8-0481-593f-bf5c-87cef9764dc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/q-333937-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220574} {:answer/id #uuid "e87b7e7b-9502-5adf-b8a0-c600b8fb6049" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/q-333937-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220575} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9d3525-0498-5269-9cbb-9c7c5d0645c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/q-333937-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220576} {:answer/id #uuid "25ad56b1-af9f-5cf4-b3fc-0f311662afd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/q-333937-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e87b7e7b-9502-5adf-b8a0-c600b8fb6049" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/q-333937-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220575} :answer-field/id #uuid "09efbcbe-980b-5204-84a9-9aaf50833e2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220572}] :question/id #uuid "2b4ec02f-d320-5430-a8ba-2d24736699c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-333937" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-x(t) - y(t); y^{′}(t) =-2x(t) - 2y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1} = 0$ and $λ_{2} =-3$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $v_{1} = [- 1, 1]^{T}$ and $v_{2} = [1, 2]^{T}$, respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*"}]}
